Abstract
Developing wound dressing that inhibits bacterial infection for treating complex wound healing processes has been a research hotspot. Here, we report the fabrication of Cu-MOFs (HKUST-1) incorporated electrospun chitosan/polyvinyl alcohol (HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA) fibers through the blending electrospinning for wound therapy. HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers displayed satisfying physical properties, such as mechanical property, water uptake, water vapor transmission rate, etc. Cytotoxicity test indicated that HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers were biocompatible and could support cell adhesion. Due to the HKUST-1 incorporation, HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers exhibited the good antibacterial activity against Escherichia coli and Staphylococcus aureus with $99\%$ antibacterial efficiency. Furthermore, in animal studies, compared with commercial chitosan dressings and chitosan/PVA fibers, HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers were more efficient to heal the wound with less inflammation. In summary, the HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers with good physicochemical property, biocompatibility and antibacterial property is an excellent wound dressing for full-thickness skin repair.
Keywords: electrospinning; chitosan; metal-organic framework; wound dressing
## 1. Introduction
Acute or chronic skin damage caused by injury or disease presents serious impact on health and even threatens people’s life [1,2]. As a result, there is a great demand for the clinical wound dressing materials. An ideal wound dressing should keep hemostasis, moist environment at the wound, prevent from infection; allow water and air permeation and promote epithelization [3]. Although studies for wound dressings have been investigated for a long time, there are still some challenges in wound healing. Among the challenges, bacterial infection has become the significant problem, which greatly affects the normal skin recovery [4]. To control wound infection, antibiotics are often used to treat the wounds [5]. However, inappropriate usage of antibiotics may lead to toxicity, allergy and development of resistance [2]. For the past few decades, metallic antibacterial agents, such as silver, zinc, copper-based ions or oxides, have been employed to kill or inhibit the bacterial growth around the wounds [6]. However, the use of metallic antibacterial agents is still risky because of their potential toxicity induced by the rapid release of metal ions [7]. Therefore, there is a great clinical need to alleviate the toxicity of metallic antibacterial agents through slowing down the metal ion release.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have raised great interest of scientific researchers in the past decade. MOFs are the new type of porous crystalline materials, which are the periodic framework structure formed by metal ions and organic ligands through the coordination interactions [8]. MOFs have the advantages of high specific surface area, lager porosity, variable structure, adjustable and modifiable channels, etc., and show many promising applications including catalysis, gas adsorption and separation, luminescence, sensors, and functional devices [9]. In addition, MOFs also show their great potential in biomedical applications including drug delivery, photodynamic therapy, bioimaging, biological detection [10-12], etc. Recently, some studies have demonstrated the antibacterial ability of MOFs (silver, cobalt, copper, and zinc based), which attribute to their gradual release of metal ion [13,14]. Due to their tunable chemical and physical properties, the metal ion release from MOFs becomes amenable to fine tuning, making MOFs become suitable antibacterial agents for wound dressing [15,16]. Several studies have shown that Cu-based MOF “HKUST-1” is promising antibacterial material for wound healing [17,18]. For example, Farha and co-workers reported that modified HKUST-1 released ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ slowly, leading to reduced toxicity and enhanced wound healing performance [17]. However, the powder form of HKUST-1 cannot be used as wound dressing directly. Thus, flexible and sensitive substrates, such as polymers and textiles, are needed to improve the stability and practicability of MOFs powder when works as wound dressing [19].
The electrospun fibrous membranes show great potential in the field of wound dressing [20,21]. Fiber membranes prepared from the versatile and simple electrospinning technology display high air permeability, high surface to volume ratio and tunable pore size. Moreover, their 3D architecture can mimic the structure of the skin extracellular matrix (ECM), which plays a pivotal role in supporting cell adhesion and proliferation. These properties make electrospun fiber membranes attract a great deal of attention in wound dressing application [22]. Many polymers (synthetic polymers and natural polymers) have been electrospun into fiber membranes as wound dressings. Among the polymers, chitosan, as a natural cationic polysaccharide, has gained much attention in the biomedical applications [23]. Due to its biocompatible, antibacterial, hemostatic and antioxidant properties, chitosan is a good candidate as wound dressing material [24]. Usually, chitosan was mixed with synthetic polymers by blend electrospinning to improve the spinnability and mechanical property [25]. Polyvinyl alcohol (PVA) is a synthetic, biocompatible and low-cost polymer with good strength, and electrospun chitosan/PVA blending fibers have been widely studied as wound dressings [26]. However, the therapeutic property of chitosan/PVA fibers couldn’t satisfy adequate wound healing. To improve the wound healing capacity, functional materials such as antibiotics, metal particles, enzyme, etc., [6,27,28] are incorporated into electrospun chitosan/PVA fibers. As outlined above, MOFs can act as the modifying agent to improve the antibacterial ability of chitosan/PVA fibers and further enhance the wound healing performance. Additionally, the composition with electrospun fibers could make MOFs be more suitable for wound dressing application. Thus, the investigation of their composition for wound healing is meaningful. To the best of our knowledge, the application of MOFs/chitosan composite fibers for wound dressing material has not been reported. Furthermore, there is a dearth of research on their physical, chemical and biological properties.
In this study, we prepared MOFs/chitosan/PVA fibers via the blending electrospinning for wound dressing application. Cu-based MOFs, referred as HKUST-1, was selected as the modifying agent. In addition to their antibacterial properties, ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions can stimulate angiogenesis and collagen deposition processes, which also help the wound healing [29]. The physical and chemical properties of obtained electrospun fiber membranes were characterized. Furthermore, in vitro cell adhesion and antibacterial performance, as well as in vivo open wound healing in rats was also evaluated. All of these results demonstrate the promising potential of HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers for wound healing application.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Polyvinyl alcohol (PVA, Mw $\mathit{\Psi}=\mathit{\Psi}$ 75,000–80,000) was purchased from Beijing Chemical Factory. Chitosan (degree of deacetylation $>$ $95\%$ , viscosity $\mathrm{>400~mPa{\cdot}s},$ ) was purchased from Energy Chemical. Trimesic acid $\mathrm{\bf~\leftmoon}\mathrm{\bf{H}}_{3}\mathrm{\bf{BTC}}$ , $98\%$ ) and $\mathrm{Cu(NO_{3})_{2}}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ (AR) were received from Aladdin. Glutalraldehyde (GA, $50\%$ ) was purchased from Tianjin Huadong Product. Escherichia coli (E. coli, ATCC 25922) and Staphylococcus aureus (S. aureus, ATCC 25923) bacteria were provided by the First Hospital Microbiology laboratory of Jilin University. All other chemicals and solvents were of analytical grade and used without further purification. Deionized water was used throughout the study.
## 2.2. Fabrication of HKUST-1
HKUST-1 was prepared according to the process reported in the literature [30].
Briefly, $0.86\mathrm{\gCu(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water and $0.22{\mathrm{~g~}}$ $\mathrm{{H_{3}B T C}}$ was dissolved in $10\mathrm{~ml~}$ ethanol. After the complete dissolution, the two solutions were mixed quickly. The mixture was stirred for 10 minutes at room temperature and then $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ DMF was added to the mixed solution. The mixed solution was transferred into a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave then was placed in an oven at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After cooling down to room temperature, the blue product was washed with water and ethanol several times, then was dried in vacuum oven at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ .
## 2.3. Preparation of electrospun HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA composite fibers
To prepare the electrospinning solution, $0.70~\mathrm{g}$ PVA and $0.30~\mathrm{g}$ chitosan were added into 10 mL $1\%$ (v/v) acetic acid. The mixture was stirred at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $8\mathrm{~h~}$ to get a transparent solution. After cooling down to room temperature, $0.10\ \mathrm{g}$ HKUST-1 was added into the solution. After sonicated for $30~\mathrm{min}$ , HKUST-1 was dispersed in the solution uniformly to obtain the electrospinning solution. For the electrospinning process, the solution was loaded into a plastic syringe which connected to the cathode of high-voltage power supply. The other electrode was attached to the collector. The distance between the cathode and the anode was $18~\mathrm{cm}$ and the potential was $15\mathrm{kV}.$ The fibers were prepared at a rate of $\ensuremath{0.4}\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mL/h}}$ and fibers were gathered from collector. The obtained fibers were put into GA solution (0.2 mL GA, $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ HCl, and $20~\mathrm{mL}$ isopropyl alcohol) for $30\mathrm{min}$ to conduct the crosslinking. Then, the crosslinked fibers were washed 3 times by water and dried under vacuum at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight before use. For comparison, electrospun chitosan/PVA fibers were prepared with the similar
process without HKUST-1 incorporation.
## 2.4. Characterization of the obtained samples
The morphology of the obtained samples was observed by a field-emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM, FEI Nova NanoSEM). The diameter distribution of the fiber samples was counted from 100 different parts of the fibers using the software Image-ProPlus. FT-IR spectra were measured on a Fourier transform infrared spectrometer (FT-IR, BRUKER VECTOR 22) from 4000 to $400~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ using pressed KBr pellets at room temperature. X-ray diffraction (XRD) data were performed by using an X-ray diffractometer (Empyrean, PANalytical B.V.) based on $\mathrm{{Cu-Ka}}$ radiation with 2 angle from $4^{\mathrm{o}}$ to ${{60}^{\mathrm{{o}}}}$ . The mechanical properties of the fiber membranes were conducted on a mechanical strength tester (YG026H, Wuhan Nationa l Instrument Co., Ltd., China) at a tensile speed of $10\ \mathrm{mm\cdotmin}^{-1}$ with the fiber membrane dimensions of length $\mathrm{\Omega}=30\ \mathrm{mm}$ and width $=10{\sim}15~\mathrm{mm}$ . Water contact angle (WCA) were collected by a contact angle analyzer (Surface Electro Optics Co., Korea) at room temperature.
## 2.5. Water uptake test
The ability of water uptake of fibers was studied based on the previous report [31]. Briefly, fiber samples ( $\mathrm{100~mg)}$ were put into phosphate buffered saline (PBS) solution $\mathrm{(pH}7.4)$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . At predetermined interval time, the samples were taken out and the excess water was removed using filter paper. Then, fiber samples were weighed. Water uptake degree was calculated using the following equation. $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{Water\uptake\degree\%}=\frac{\mathbf{W}_{\mathbf{w}}-\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{d}}}{\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{d}}}\times100\$ (1) where $\mathrm{w}_{\mathrm{w}}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaW_{d}}$ are weights $\mathrm{(mg)}$ of the wet and dried fibers, respectively.
## 2.6. Water vapor transmission rates (WVTR)
Water vapor transmission rates (WVTR) were measured according to ASTM E96 (American Society for Testing Materials)-standard test method [32]. A cylindrical vessel with $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water was covered with the fiber membrane at the top. The vessel was placed into the environmental chamber at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $40\pm2\%$ relative humidity. The weight of the assembly was recorded at regular intervals of every one hour at the end of 24 hours and the slope was calculated.
$$
W T R=\frac{s l o p e\times24}{A}g/m^{2}/d a y
$$
where A is the test area of the sample in ${\mathrm{m}}^{2}$ . The water vapor evaporation studies were carried out at least in quadruplets $(\mathrm{n}25)$ .
## 2.7. In vitro antibacterial ability assays
The antibacterial abilities of obtained fibers were evaluated by bacterial colony counting method [33]. The Gram-negative bacteria $E.$ . coli and the Gram-positive bacteria S. aureus were selected as bacterium models. Bacteria cells were cultured using the Luria-Bertani (LB) nutrient broth at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a speed of $200~\mathrm{rpm}$ in a Shaker overnight, and then collected at the exponential phase by centrifugation at $5000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for 5 min. Subsequently the bacterial cells were washed with 0.01 M PBS (three times) to remove residual LB broth. After that, the bacteria cells were dispersed in 0.01 M PBS with a concentration about $1\times10^{6}\mathrm{CFU/mL}$ . Then, fiber samples with diameter of $1~\mathrm{cm}$ and $2\ \mathrm{cm}$ were added into the bacterial suspensions, which were incubated at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ . Later $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the mixed solution was cultured with LB agar for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The blank groups were the bacterial suspensions without any fiber samples. The kill ratio for the bacteria was counted by the colony-forming units on the agar plate and calculated based on the following equation: $\mathrm{Kill~ratio\%=\frac{A-B}{A}\times100\%}$ (3) where A and $\mathbf{B}$ are the number of colonies from the bacteria in the suspensions with blank groups and the sample groups, respectively.
## 2.8. In vitro cytotoxicity and cell observation
The cytotoxicity of the fiber samples was examined by 3-(4,5-dimethylthiazol-2-yl)-2,5-diphenyltetrazolium bromide (MTT) assay using L929 fibroblast cells [33]. Before the measurement, the fiber mats (with a diameter of 1 cm) were sterilized with UV radiation. L929 fibroblast cells were seeded in DMEM with $10\%$ FBS and $1\%$ v/v antibiotic in 24-well tissue culture plate (TCP) at a density of 10,000 cells/well and then fiber mats were added into the cell cultures at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $5\%$ $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ atmosphere for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ and $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The medium was replaced every day throughout the experiments. At predetermined time point, the supernatant of each well was replaced with MTT diluted in serum-free medium. After $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ ’s incubation at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , dimethyl sulfoxide was added to each well to solubilize the insoluble formazan yielded. The negative control group was incubated with $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ culture medium DMEM and the positive control group was incubated with $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}\ 5\%$ DMSO under the same conditions. All the experiments were conducted in triplicates. The cell viability was determined by measuring the absorbance at $490~\mathrm{nm}$ on a microplate reader (Multiskan MK3) and calculated based on the following formula:
Cell viability% = Anegastiavmepcloentrol $\begin{array}{r}{\gamma\%=\frac{\mathsf{A}_{s\mathrm{ample}}}{\mathsf{A}_{\mathrm{negative\control}}}\times100\%}\end{array}$ (4) where $\mathbf{A}_{\mathrm{sample}}$ and Anegative control are the absorptions at $490\mathrm{nm}$ for the experimental and negative control wells, respectively.
For fluorescence microscopic analysis, after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ ’s incubation, $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Calcein-AM 0 $30\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , Sigma) was added to each well, followed by the incubation at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $5\%$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ in dark. After the fibers was washed with PBS gently for 2 times, the cell attachment was observed under the fluorescence microscope with a Nikon Eclipse LV100 microscope attached to a B-2A filter at an excitation wavelength of 445-495 nm.
2.9. In vivo animal experiment.
A full-thickness excisional wound model was used to evaluate the wound healing efficacy of dressings. Animal experiments were approved by the ethical committee of Jilin University’s guidelines. Animal experiments were conducted under direct suggestion of care for laboratory animals approved by the Ministry of Science and Technology of China. We chose adult male balb/c rats (6\~8 months old) purchased from the Laboratory Animal Centre of Jilin University, each rat was caged individually under standard conditions of controlled temperatures $(23~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and a light/dark cycle $(12/12\mathrm{h})$ ) with free access to water and food. For surgical procedure, the animals were anesthetized by intraperitoneal injection of $10\%$ chloral hydrate (at $0\%\ 0.3\mathrm{ml3mL/100g\kg}$ body weight). The dorsal hair of each rat was shaved and a full-thickness $1.5{\times}1.5~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ wound to depth of loose subcutaneous tissue was excised using a scalpel blade. The animals were randomly and evenly divided into four groups (A-D). A group was the blank control, B group was treated with commercial chitosan wound dressing (Zhengzhou Kangjinrui Health Industry co., ltd, China), C group was treated with chitosan/PVA fibers, D group was treated with HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers. The healing process was recorded during the wound recover. For histological examination, the skin tissue samples were isolated and then fixed with $10\%$ formalin. After the samples were embedded by paraffin, sectioned, stained via hematoxylin and eosin (H&E), the samples were observed by an optical microscope.
## 2.10. Statistical analysis
All experiments data were exhibited as the mean value ± standard deviation (SD). Statistically significant value was assessed on Student’s t test and P-values $<0.05$ was regarded as statistically significant.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Fiber preparation and morphology
of chitosan/PVA fibers (a, c) and HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers (b, d).
Cu based MOFs (HKUST-1) were synthesized through the solvothermal reaction according to the previous report [30]. The size of the HKUST-1 particles could be controlled by the reactant concentration and reaction time. Our prepared HKUST-1 particles are shown in Scheme 1 and their SEM image is shown in Fig. S1. The particle size was $310~\mathrm{nm}$ , which can match the electrospun fibers’ diameters well and is beneficial to the incorporation. For comparison, electrospun chitosan/PVA fibers without HKUST-1 were also prepared. The morphologies of chitosan/PVA fibers and
HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers were observed using SEM. As shown in Fig. 1, all the samples had long and continuous fiber morphology. Chitosan/PVA fibers showed smooth surfaces with an average diameter of $443\ \mathrm{nm}$ . After the incorporation of HKUST-1 into the fibers, the average diameters increased to $518\mathrm{nm}$ . The rising in the diameter of fibers was owned to the improved concentration of electrospinning solution after adding HKUST-1 particles. The color of the fibers after HKUST-1 incorporation changed into blue from white. In comparison with chitosan/PVA fibers, HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers’ surfaces turned relatively coarse and aggregated particles could be observed on the fibers. Except for the surface, the particles could also be observed in the fibers. As shown in the elemental mapping image (Fig. S2), Cu element well distributed across the fibers, indicating that HKUST-1 particles are incorporated into the fibers uniformly. The 3D porous and fibrous structures with interconnected pores of the obtained electrospun fibers could act as a good platform for cell adhesion and proliferation.
## 3.2. Chemical composition characterization
The chemical structures of obtained fiber samples were investigated by FT-IR spectra to verify their chemical bond structures and the results are shown in Fig. 2a. Chitosan/PVA fibers and HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers displayed typical peaks of chitosan and PVA. The broad peak at around $3400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was assigned to the N–H stretch and O–H stretch from PVA and chitosan. The obvious peaks at $2942~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $2897~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ belonged to the asymmetric and symmetric stretching vibration of $-\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ group in the polymer chains [34]. The peaks at $1631~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ was due to the amide I of chitosan and the peaks at $1556~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ was due to the amide $\mathrm{II}$ as well as the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ bending [35]. The peaks observed at $1000{-}1460~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were attributed to the vibrations for the C–OH group and C–C skeletal vibrations in PVA [35]. However, in comparison with pure PVA and chitosan, those two fibers showed differences in some characteristic peaks. The intensities of peaks at $3400~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $1556~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ decreased and the intensities of peaks at $1631~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $1181~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ (–C–O–C– vibration) increased [26]. These results are due to the crosslinking reactions between glutaraldehyde and chitosan (or PVA), which happens between aldehyde groups and the hydroxyl, amino groups to form ether bond and imine bond. The crosslinking process makes the fibers insoluble in water, which can be used as wound dressings. In addition, HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers showed some new peaks. The peak at 1721 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponded to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ stretching vibration from $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBTC}$ and the peak at 707 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponded to the C–O stretching. These are the characteristic peaks of HKUST-1 [36]. To further verify the structure of HKUST-1, XRD was carried out (Fig. 2b). Chitosan/PVA fibers showed one diffraction peak at $20.1^{\mathrm{o}}$ corresponded to the amorphous polymers [33]. HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers displayed typical diffraction peaks of HKUST-1, which is in accordance with the HKUST-1 powder and the standard simulated XRD pattern for HKUST-1. No significant changes in crystallinity were observed. These chemical characterization results suggest that HKUST-1 particles have been incorporated into chitosan/PVA fibers successfully and the electrospinning process doesn’t change the structure of HKUST-1.
## 3.3. Physical property characterization
The physical properties, such as mechanical property, water vapor transmission rate, water uptake behavior, etc., are important for wound dressing materials. The related properties of obtained fibers were studied and the results are shown in Fig. 3. The tensile stress-strain curves were used to study the mechanical properties of obtained fibers (Fig. S3). The results are summarized in Fig. 3a and 3b. The tensile strength for chitosan/PVA fibers was $4.5{\pm}0.7$ MPa and the elongation at break was $14.6{\pm}1.9\%$ .
The results agree with the previous reports. After the HKUST-1 incorporation, the tensile strength decreased to $4.0{\pm}1.1\$ MPa and the elongation at break decrease to $11.2{\pm}2.3\%$ . The reduced mechanical properties are due to the inorganic fragile MOF in the fibers which can block the continuity of the organic polymers. However, the mechanical property of HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers can meet the requirement of dressing usage [37]. The suitable mechanical property can maintain their integrity, increase the comfort level and avoid the secondary damage during the practical applications. The water uptake behavior of scaffolds is also important for wound dressing as good water uptake behavior can make dressings absorb the exudates around the wound. The water uptake of obtained fibers is evaluated by the water absorption performance (Fig. 3c). The water uptake for the two fibers increased gradually until reached equilibrium at $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The maximum water uptake degree for chitosan/PVA fibers was $202.3\%$ . Moreover, the water uptake behavior of HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers was better than that of chitosan/PVA fibers and the maximum water uptake degree was $235.3\%$ . The abundant micropores in HKUST-1 can make more water molecules be adsorbed. The good water uptake can also provide favorable conditions for cell adhesion and spreading. The water vapor transmission rate (WVTR) of the dressings should be appropriate to control the water loss evaporated from a wound. According to the previous literature, water vapor transmission rate of a wound dressing depends on materials used, must be in the range of $76{\cdot}9360~\mathrm{g/m^{2}/d a y}$ . The water vapor loss from normal skin is $204{\pm}12\ \mathrm{g/m}^{2}/\mathrm{day}$ from first degree burn is $279{\pm}26\ \mathrm{g/m}^{2}/\mathrm{day}$ , and from granulation wound is $5138{\pm}202$ $\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{m}^{2}.$ /day and recommended rate to provide an adequate level of moisture on wound surface for effective wound healing is $2000{-}2500\ \mathrm{g/m^{2}/d\it{s}}$ y [32]. A higher WVTR dried the wound more quickly and produced the scars. Moreover, a lower WVTR accumulated exudates, which might retard the healing process and result in increased risk of bacterial growth. As shown in Fig. 4d, the WVTR of chitosan/PVA fibers and HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers are ca. 2856 and $2601~\mathrm{g/m}^{2}/\mathrm{i}$ ay, respectively. These values are close to the recommended range of an ideal wound dressing, suggesting that fiber dressings can provide the appropriate moisture in the wound environment. The above results indicate that the obtained fibers serve as a suitable platform for diffusion of essential materials for cell growth and are suitable for wound dressing application.
## 3.4. Antibacterial ability
fibers. The kill ratios from colony count evaluation against $E$ . coli (b) and S. aureus
Wounds are always accompanied by bacterial infections, which low wound healing, even cause fever, bacteremia and other serious infections. Thus, antibacterial activity is very needful for wound dressings, which was studied by using bacterial colony count method against Escherichia coli (E. coli) and Staphylococcus aureus (S. aureus) bacterial strains as a model of gram-negative and gram-positive bacteria, respectively. As shown in Fig. 4a, chitosan/PVA fibers showed antibacterial properties against the both bacteria. The kill ratio increased with the increase of fiber dosage. For the chitosan/PVA fibers with diameter of $2\ \mathrm{cm}$ , the kill ratio against $E$ . coli was $58.9\%$ and the kill ratio for S. aureus was $67.4\%$ . The antibacterial ability of chitosan/PVA fibers is attributed to chitosan, which has been proven by many studies [6,28,33]. However, the antibacterial ability of chitosan/PVA fibers couldn’t give the wound adequate treatment. Thus, HKUST-1 was incorporated into chitosan/PVA fibers to enhance the antibacterial activity. It can be seen that the antibacterial ability was obviously improved after the HKUST-1 incorporation. For the HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers with diameter of $2\ \mathrm{cm}$ , the kill ratios against both bacteria were over $99.0\%$ . The antibacterial ability of HKUST-1 was due to its released ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions [17]. The good antibacterial property of HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers from the synergistic antibacterial effect of HKUST-1 and chitosan can provide the wound a sterile environment and prevent its infection.
## 3.5. In vitro cytotoxicity and cell adhesion
Biocompatibility is a necessary property for wound dressing materials. The cytotoxicity of obtained fibers was evaluated with MTT assay against L929 fibroblast cells. As shown in Fig. 5a, in comparison with negative control group, the cell viabilities for chitosan/PVA fibers were over $100\%$ during the incubation time, suggesting that chitosan/PVA fibers can enhance the proliferation of cell. The results are in accordance with the previous reports that chitosan and PVA possess good biocompatibility [6]. HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers showed lower cell viabilities than chitosan/PVA fibers because of the released ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions from HKUST-1. However, the cell viabilities for HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers were still over $90\%$ . The cell viabilities for positive control groups were below $32\%$ . Moreover, statistical differences between experimental groups and positive control groups were significant and the statistical differences between experimental groups and negative control groups were not significant. The results indicated that HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers have no obvious cytotoxicity and HKUST-1 could be used as the addition agent in wound dressings, which is in accordance with the previous studies [17,18]. The incorporation of HKUST-1 into electrospun fibers could reduce the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ion release rate due to the barrier of polymer substrate. To investigate cell viability further, the cell adhesion onto the fibers was observed by fluorescence micrographic images. As shown in Fig. 5b\~5d, except for the quantity difference, there was no significant difference in cell morphology compared with the negative control group. The cells on the fibers were healthy. These results confirmed that the obtained fibers are nontoxic and can act as the platform for cell attachment and proliferation, which is meaningful for wound dressing materials.
## 3.6. In vivo wound healing evaluation and histological analysis
On the basis of the above physical, chemical and biological properties, the full-thickness skin wounds in rats were treated with HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers to evaluate their efficacy visually. Blank, commercial chitosan dressing and chitosan/PVA fibers were employed as the controls. The whole process of wound healing was recorded at 6 and 12 d, respectively (Fig. 6). In comparison with the blank group, wounds treated with dressing materials showed better healing effect, suggesting that wound dressings could indeed promote the wound closure. In comparison with commercial chitosan dressing, the wound healing treated with chitosan/PVA fibers was better, which is due to the fibrous morphology of electrospun fibers. The structure is similar to the micromorphology of the native extracellular matrix that is beneficial to the wound healing. Among the three dressing groups,
HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers exhibited fastest healing rate. On the 12th day, the wound closures for commercial chitosan dressing and chitosan/PVA fibers were $89.3\%$ and $90.7\%$ , respectively; and the wound closure HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers was $99.1\%$ . Moreover, the wound treated with HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers was much flatter after the healing without red and uneven scars. The better wound healing effect of HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers was attributed to the HKUST-1 incorporation. Except for the antibacterial effects, the released ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions can promote collagen deposition and stimulate angiogenesis, which can accelerate the wound healing.
24th day (scale bar is $200~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Black rectangle, yellow rectangle, blue circle, yellow circle, blue arrow, and black dashed line indicate disorder fibrous tissue, inflammatory cells, scab, follicle and sebaceous gland, fibroblasts, and boundary of epithelium and dermis, respectively.).
The effect of the dressings on wound healing was further examined by H&E staining (Fig. 7). On day 6, the wounds were still exposed due to their unclosed. For blank group, fibrous tissue was disordered. For the other dressing groups, fibroblasts and inflammatory cells were observed around the wounds. However, HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fiber groups showed less inflammatory cells and more fibroblast cells in the region, which is attributed to the efficacy of HKUST-1. On day 12, obvious scabs could be observed in the blank group and chitosan/PVA fiber group. For the other dressing groups, the regeneration processes have been built. In comparison with chitosan dressing group, wounds treated with HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers have completed the re-epithelialization process, and the epidermal layer was well structured. In addition, HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fiber group showed abundant hair follicles and sebaceous glands. The H&E staining results are consistent with the wound appearances in Fig. 6a, indicating that HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers can serve as an antimicrobial wound dressing.
## 4. Conclusions
In this study, we demonstrated the HKUST-1 incorporation into electrospun chitosan/PVA fibers via the blending electrospinning. HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers showed satisfying physical properties for wound healing. Moreover, HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers could support cell adhesion and proliferation. In addition, due to the incorporation of HKUST-1, HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers showed good antibacterial activity against $E$ . coli and S. aureus. Consequently, in a rat wound model, the HKUST-1/chitosan/PVA fibers accelerated wound healing with less inflammation outperformed other groups. In summary, we propose that the incorporation of MOFs into electrospun fibers are particularly promising candidates for skin tissue regeneration.
